summary:
  A loopback built by the enable or a rebuild is left open to whatever level
  the session manager remembered under its name.

history:
  WirePlumber files a stream's level under "loopback-<pid>-<n> output" and
  restores it onto the next stream of that name. The counter cycles and a
  lingering login reuses the pid, so names repeat — and a handover fades its
  old loopback to zero before cutting it, which is the level that stays on
  disk. Measured 2026-09-20 with 0.0 stored under loopback-1322-15: a fresh
  loopback came up at -inf dB, unmuted and running. With the property the
  same name came up at full level and nothing set on it was written back.
  The two load sites are separate strings, so each needs its own watch.

target:
  package/contents/ui/SyncEngine.qml

find:
                   + " sink='" + s + "' latency_msec=" + d + " " + chSpec + _lbNoRestore
                   + ") && echo \"LB $id " + s + "\"";

replace:
                   + " sink='" + s + "' latency_msec=" + d + " " + chSpec
                   + ") && echo \"LB $id " + s + "\"";

expect:
  Killed by tst_syncengine::test_no_loopback_is_born_at_a_level_the_session_manager_remembered.
  Line-neutral.
